          <a href="#whole-home" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Which Rooms</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, in most homes you should run an air purifier 24/7, on auto or a low setting, because a room's particle levels climb back toward where they started within an hour or two of switching it off, and a typical unit costs only about $1 to $6 a month in electricity to run nonstop. The real trade-offs are faster filter wear and, in a bedroom, noise, and both are solved by choosing a unit big enough to do its job quietly. There are a handful of situations where switching it off makes sense, mostly involving ozone-producing features, a damp or moldy filter, or windows open for ventilation. Here is how to decide, and how to run one well.
        </p>

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Is Running It Continuously Better Than a Few Hours a Day?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because the air in a room never stops getting dirty. People shed skin flakes and stir up dust just by walking around. Pets shed dander. Cooking puts fine particles into the air, and outdoor pollution and pollen leak in through gaps, doors and the HVAC system. An air purifier does not clean a room once and leave it clean; it holds the room at a lower level for as long as it runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switch it off and that level starts to rise. How fast depends on the house, but in a typical lived-in room the particle count can drift back toward its starting point within an hour or two. A purifier that runs for a couple of hours in the evening gives you clean air for those hours and a little after, and ordinary air the rest of the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            For allergies and asthma that matters. Symptoms respond to the total exposure over days and weeks, not to an hour of clean air, so the benefit builds when the purifier keeps levels consistently low. That is also why the bedroom is the most important place to run one continuously: it is where you spend the longest unbroken stretch of time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Continuous running also catches events you would not think to switch it on for: the burst of particles when someone makes the bed, the smoke from a pan left on too long, a neighbor's fireplace on a still night. In auto mode the purifier reacts to those within minutes, which is something an occasional schedule simply cannot do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Does It Cost to Run an Air Purifier Nonstop?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually a few dollars a month. Most home purifiers draw roughly 5 to 20 watts on low or auto and 30 to 60 watts on high for a mid-size unit. Running continuously, a unit averaging 10 watts uses about 7 kilowatt-hours a month, which is around $1.20 at about 16 to 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, close to the US average; check your own rate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How You Run It</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Average Draw</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Monthly Cost (24/7, around US average rate)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sleep or lowest speed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 3 to 10 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.35 to $1.20</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Auto mode, typical home</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8 to 20 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1 to $2.40</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Medium all day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15 to 35 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.80 to $4.20</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High all day, mid-size unit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 to 60 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.60 to $7.10</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those are modest numbers next to the appliances that heat, cool or dehumidify a home, which draw hundreds or thousands of watts. Our full breakdown of <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-an-air-purifier-use/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how much electricity an air purifier uses</a> covers the math for your own rate, standby power and Energy Star models.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger running cost is filters, not electricity. That is the trade-off worth thinking about, and it is covered in the next section. But if the electricity bill is the reason you are switching your purifier off, the savings are small and the cost in air quality is large.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Will Running It 24/7 Wear Out the Filter Faster?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. A filter's life is set by how much air and dirt pass through it, so a purifier running all day loads its filter faster than one running a few hours. That is not a flaw; a filter that lasts a long time because the purifier is usually off is a filter that is not cleaning your air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most manufacturers quote filter life on the assumption of regular daily use, and typical ranges are 6 to 12 months for a HEPA filter and 3 to 6 months for a carbon filter. Some base the figure on a set number of hours a day, so check the manual if you run yours continuously. Homes with pets, smokers, heavy cooking or a renovation underway will land at the short end. Our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-change-air-purifier-filter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to change an air purifier filter</a> covers the signs and schedules.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can stretch filter life without switching off. Vacuum or rinse the washable pre-filter every few weeks, because it catches the hair and lint that would otherwise clog the HEPA layer. Run on auto or low rather than high, which moves less air through the filter when the room is already clean. And size the purifier so it is not straining on a high setting all day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for it up front. Look up the price of replacement filters before buying a purifier and multiply by how many you will need in a year of continuous use. A unit with affordable, easy-to-find filters is the one you will actually keep running.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="night" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is It Safe and Comfortable to Run It All Night?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. A certified air purifier is designed to run continuously, the same way a refrigerator or a fan is, and running it overnight is one of the best uses of one. Look for a safety certification mark from a recognized testing lab, keep the cord out of walkways, do not run it through an extension cord that is warm to the touch, and leave clearance around the vents so it does not overheat or starve itself of air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is the practical issue. Most purifiers are quiet on their lowest settings, roughly in the range of a soft whisper, and loud on high. A unit that is only effective on high will end up switched off at bedtime. The fix is to buy a purifier with enough capacity that its low or medium setting still delivers useful clean air for your bedroom, then use sleep mode or a fixed low speed overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Light can be a problem too. Bright displays and color-changing air quality rings are irritating in a dark room. Most units let you dim or turn off the display, and sleep mode usually does it automatically. Put the purifier where its outlet does not blow directly on you, a few feet from the bed with clear space around it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good routine is to run the purifier on high with the bedroom door closed for 30 to 60 minutes before bed, then switch to sleep or low for the night. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-it-take-an-air-purifier-to-clean-a-room/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long an air purifier takes to clean a room</a> explains the math behind that window.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="auto" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Should You Use Auto Mode or a Fixed Low Speed?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Auto mode is the best default during the day. It uses a built-in particle sensor to sit on a low speed when the air is clean and ramp up when it detects cooking smoke, dust or anything else in the air. You get high-speed cleaning when it matters and low power and noise the rest of the time, without having to think about it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Auto has limits. The sensor sits right at the purifier, so it reacts to the air nearest the unit, and most sensors detect particles rather than gases, so they will not respond to odors or VOCs. Some cheaper units also have sensors that are slow or poorly calibrated, so auto mode either rarely ramps up or ramps up constantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            At night, a fixed low or sleep setting is often better. Auto mode can jump to a loud speed at 3 a.m. because someone rolled over in a dusty duvet, which is exactly when you do not want it. If your unit has a sleep mode that caps the speed, use it; if not, pick the highest fixed setting you can comfortably sleep through.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not sure whether your purifier is doing enough on auto or low, a cheap PM2.5 monitor across the room settles it. If the reading stays low and steady, the setting is fine. If it hovers high, the purifier is undersized for the room or the setting is too low.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="turn-off" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Should You Turn an Air Purifier Off?
          </h2>

          <p>
            When it has an ozone-producing feature switched on. Some purifiers include ionizers, electrostatic stages, plasma or photocatalytic modules that can produce ozone, a lung irritant. If yours has a separate switch for those features, leave them off and run the filter alone. Our guide to <a href="/do-air-purifiers-produce-ozone/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether air purifiers produce ozone</a> explains which technologies to watch for, and devices sold as ozone generators should never run in occupied rooms, as covered in our look at <a href="/do-ozone-generators-kill-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether ozone generators kill mold</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the filter is damp, moldy or missing. If the unit starts blowing musty air, switch it off, check the filters and replace anything with spots or a damp smell before running it again. Running without a filter just moves dust around. Our guide on <a href="/can-mold-grow-in-an-air-purifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether mold can grow in an air purifier</a> explains the warning signs.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you are airing the room out. With the windows wide open on a clean-air day, the purifier is trying to clean the outdoors and adds little. Switch it to low or off while you ventilate, then back on when the windows close. The reverse applies on smoky or high-pollen days: windows shut and the purifier on high.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going away for a long trip is a judgment call. Switching off saves a little electricity and filter life. Leaving it on auto keeps the air clean for pets and avoids coming home to stale air. In a damp basement, leaving it running keeps air moving through the filter, which is better than letting it sit damp and still for weeks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-home" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Do You Need a Purifier Running in Every Room?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. A portable purifier cleans one room well, and the most valuable room is the bedroom, where you spend seven or eight hours in one place with the door usually closed. If you are buying one purifier, put it there and run it continuously. A second unit in the main living space is the next most useful addition.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the rest of the house, the HVAC system does more than a fleet of small purifiers. If you have forced air, a MERV 11 to 13 filter that your system can handle cleans air from every room whenever the fan runs. Setting the thermostat fan to on or circulate, rather than auto, keeps it filtering continuously, though it uses more power than a portable purifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sensitive households can go further. For allergies or asthma, a purifier in each bedroom plus a good HVAC filter covers the rooms where exposure matters most. Our picks for the <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for allergies</a> focus on true HEPA units that are quiet enough to run around the clock in a bedroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, size it so continuous running is comfortable. A unit whose low setting already gives the room four or more air changes an hour will be quiet, cheap to run and effective, which is the combination that keeps it switched on. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for home</a> are ranked with that in mind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it only when the air seems bad</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                By the time you notice dust or smell smoke, you have been breathing it for a while, and the room starts getting dirty again within an hour or two of switching off. Leave the purifier on auto all day so it catches spikes as they happen and holds the room at a consistently lower level.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a unit that is only effective on high</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A purifier that has to run on its loudest setting to keep up will be switched off at bedtime and whenever you are on the phone. Size up so a quiet low or medium setting delivers enough clean air, and continuous running becomes something you barely notice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the ionizer switched on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ionizer, plasma and similar features on some purifiers can produce ozone, which irritates the lungs. If your unit has a separate switch, turn those features off and let the HEPA filter do the work. If it does not, check for California Air Resources Board certification before running it nonstop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting that filters wear faster</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Continuous running loads filters faster, so a schedule set for occasional use will leave you with a clogged, ineffective filter. Check the pre-filter every few weeks, inspect the main filter regularly, and budget for HEPA replacements every 6 to 12 months and carbon every 3 to 6.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it next to an open window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With windows wide open, the purifier is trying to clean outdoor air and achieves little. On clean-air days, ventilate with the purifier low or off, then close up and turn it back on. On smoky or high-pollen days, keep the windows shut and run it on high.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to leave an air purifier on all the time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Air purifiers are designed for continuous use, and running one nonstop is how they deliver the most benefit. The only real downsides are faster filter wear and a few dollars a month in electricity. Make sure it carries a recognized safety certification, keep clearance around the vents, and switch off any ozone-producing ionizer feature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many hours a day should I run an air purifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ideally all of them. If you must limit it, prioritize the hours you are in the room, especially overnight in the bedroom, and start it 30 to 60 minutes before you arrive so the air is already clean. A few hours a day gives only a few hours of benefit, because particle levels rise again soon after it stops.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I turn my air purifier off when I leave the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, and it saves a little electricity and filter life. The trade-off is coming home to air that has drifted back to normal levels. A better approach for most people is to leave it on auto, which drops to a low, cheap speed when nobody is stirring up dust. Pets at home are another reason to leave it running.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will running an air purifier all night dry out the air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A filter-based air purifier only moves and filters air; it does not remove moisture the way a dehumidifier or air conditioner does. The air movement can feel slightly cooling, like a gentle fan, but humidity stays the same. If the air feels dry in winter, the cause is usually your heating, not the purifier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does running an air purifier 24/7 shorten its lifespan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A little, in the sense that the fan motor accumulates more hours, but purifier motors are built for continuous duty and usually outlast years of use. Filters are the part that wears, and they are meant to be replaced. Keep the intake clear, clean the pre-filter regularly, and the unit itself should last for years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I run an air purifier with the windows open?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not on clean-air days. With windows wide open the purifier is effectively trying to filter the outdoors. Ventilate for a while with it low or off, then close up and run it. During wildfire smoke or high pollen, keep the windows shut and run the purifier on high instead, with doors closed to the rooms you use most.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it OK to run an air purifier and a humidifier at the same time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but keep them a few feet apart so the purifier does not pull in very humid air, and use distilled water in ultrasonic humidifiers to avoid mineral dust that the purifier then has to catch. Keep room humidity at or below about 50 percent, which helps prevent mold and dust mites.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

