          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most homes the answer is simple: leave the air purifier on, on auto during the day and a quiet setting at night. A room's air starts sliding back to normal within an hour or two of switching off, and the electricity to prevent that costs a few dollars a month. Filter wear is the real cost, and it is the cost of the purifier doing its job.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Switch it off when an ozone-producing feature is running, when the filter is damp or musty, or when the windows are wide open on a clean-air day. Otherwise, buy one big enough to run quietly, put it in the bedroom first, and let it run.
          </p>
